Fix the two GCC 14 -Wincompatible-pointer-types hard errors.

GCC 14 promoted -Wincompatible-pointer-types from a warning to an error by
default. ion-c 1.1.3 has two sites that trip it, so the port does not build at
all on a GCC 14 toolchain (Debian 13 trixie, which ships g++/gcc 14 and has no
gcc-13 package). CI builds this project under g++-13 and clang++-18, where both
sites are warnings at most, so CI never sees either one.

Both fixes are behaviour-preserving; neither changes what a correct build does.

1. ionc/ion_allocation.c, _ion_strdup(): the memcpy source argument is a
   conditional expression whose arms are "\0" (char *) and src->value (BYTE *,
   i.e. unsigned char *). Those are different pointer types, so the conditional
   has no composite type. Casting the literal arm to const BYTE * makes both
   arms point to the same type; the result is const BYTE *, which memcpy takes
   as its const void * source without complaint. Pure type repair -- the bytes
   copied are identical.

2. ionc/ion_binary.c, ion_binary_read_int_64_and_sign(): ION_GET(pstream, b)
   expands, on its slow path, to ion_stream_read_byte(pstream, &b). That
   function's second parameter is int *, but b here is declared uint64_t --
   this is the one ION_GET call site in the whole library whose variable is not
   an int. So the callee writes an int through a uint64_t *, which both
   violates strict aliasing and initialises only 4 of b's 8 bytes. It is a real
   latent bug, not merely a pedantic type mismatch, and it is only luck (b being
   zero-initialised, plus little-endian layout) that it behaves at all today.

   Fixed by reading into an int and widening explicitly, which is what every
   other ION_GET site already does. b stays uint64_t because the code below
   needs the 64-bit width for `b <<= (len * 8)`.

   This does not change behaviour even at EOF, where ion_stream_read_byte sets
   *p_c = EOF (-1) and still returns IERR_OK. The three candidate readings of
   that -1 -- the current truncating write (0x00000000FFFFFFFF), sign-extension
   (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF), and byte zero-extension (0xFF) -- all have bit 0x80
   set, so `*isNegative` is TRUE in every case, and the immediately following
   `b &= 0x7f` collapses all three to 0x7f before b is used again. For any
   non-EOF read the value is 0..255 and all three readings are identical
   anyway.

diff --git a/ionc/ion_allocation.c b/ionc/ion_allocation.c
index f92d430..90000e6 100644
--- a/ionc/ion_allocation.c
+++ b/ionc/ion_allocation.c
@@ -92,7 +92,7 @@ iERR _ion_strdup(hOWNER owner, iSTRING dst, iSTRING src)
         dst->value = (BYTE *)ion_alloc_with_owner(owner, (is_empty) ? 1 : src->length);
         if (!dst->value) FAILWITH(IERR_NO_MEMORY);
     }
-    memcpy(dst->value, (is_empty) ? "\0" : src->value, (is_empty) ? 1 : src->length);
+    memcpy(dst->value, (is_empty) ? (const BYTE *)"\0" : src->value, (is_empty) ? 1 : src->length);
 
     dst->length = src->length;
 
diff --git a/ionc/ion_binary.c b/ionc/ion_binary.c
index d8d8650..01258d9 100644
--- a/ionc/ion_binary.c
+++ b/ionc/ion_binary.c
@@ -338,13 +338,15 @@ iERR ion_binary_read_int_64_and_sign(ION_STREAM *pstream, int32_t len, uint64_t
     iENTER;
     uint64_t unsignedValue = 0;
     uint64_t b = 0;
+    int      b_byte = 0;
 
     ASSERT(p_value);
     ASSERT(isNegative != NULL);
 
     if (len) {
         // read the first byte, it's special since it carries the sign
-        ION_GET(pstream, b);
+        ION_GET(pstream, b_byte);
+        b = (uint64_t)b_byte;
         len--;
         if ((*isNegative = (b & 0x80) != 0)) {
             b &= 0x7f;
